Raw vault versus business vault.

A mature Data Vault has two layers, and keeping them separate is what protects the vault's audit guarantee. And the distinction comes down to one thing: has a business rule been applied yet?

First, the raw vault. It holds source data as-is — loaded straight from the source, with no business rules, no cleansing, no interpretation. If the source sent it, the raw vault stores it, verbatim, forever. This is the system of record: because nothing is ever transformed or overwritten, you can always prove exactly what a source said, and when. The Jabra hubs, links, and satellites you've seen are the raw vault — the immutable integration layer.

Then, the business vault. It's a thin layer built on top of the raw vault, holding data that results from applying business logic. That includes computed, or derived, satellites — a cleansed address, a standardized name, a calculated margin. It includes derived links — a relationship the source didn't state explicitly, but the business infers. And it includes other business-rule results — de-duplication, classifications, KPIs. It uses the same hub, link, and satellite structures — it's just downstream of the raw layer, and derived, rather than source-verbatim.

So why split them? Three reasons. Auditability stays intact — the raw vault is pristine, so you never lose the original just because a rule was applied. Rules can change without reloading — if a business rule is wrong, or evolves, you simply rebuild the business vault from the untouched raw vault; the system of record never moves. And interpretation is isolated — everyone can see where raw fact ends and business opinion begins. Keep the business vault sparse — add to it only what the raw vault can't answer directly. And then the information mart, which is the next section, is built from the raw and business vaults together. So: the raw vault stores source data verbatim — the immutable system of record; the business vault, built on top, holds rule-derived data. Separating them keeps the audit trail pristine, and lets business logic change without ever touching history.
